v3d: flush the MMU before each bin and render job on V3D < 4.1

On the KSTB6077's V3D 3.3, dEQP-GLES2 flush_finish.wait faulted in about a
third of the runs: "MMU error from client ? (0x24) ... pte invalid" at the
first page of the render job's RCL BO, then a GPU reset (V3D_ERR_STAT
0x1000). At the fault the PTE for that page was valid, and the BO had been
mapped tens of milliseconds earlier while the previous job was running, so
the MMU still used a stale translation despite the flush in
v3d_mmu_insert_ptes(). With unused PTEs pointed at the scratch page instead,
the faults went away but the resets stayed (the CLE read zeros).

The closed driver (nexus.ko, BVC5_P_HardwarePrepareForJob) clears the MMU
right before it starts a job. Do the same before the bin and render jobs on
V3D < 4.1. Tested: flush_finish.wait 0 failing runs of 12 with the flush,
2 of 6 without it, same kernel and boot.

--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/v3d/v3d_sched.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/v3d/v3d_sched.c
@@ -65,6 +65,19 @@
 	return container_of(sched_job, struct v3d_cpu_job, base.base);
 }
 
+/* Before V3D 4.1 a page mapped while another job runs can still read as
+ * unmapped when a later job first uses it: "pte invalid" although the PTE
+ * is valid, then a hang. The flush in v3d_mmu_insert_ptes() is not enough
+ * (likely a page walk in flight across it). The closed driver flushes the
+ * MMU before it starts a job; do the same.
+ */
+static void
+v3d_mmu_flush_before_job(struct v3d_dev *v3d)
+{
+	if (v3d->ver < V3D_GEN_41)
+		v3d_mmu_flush_all(v3d);
+}
+
 static void
 v3d_sched_job_free(struct drm_sched_job *sched_job)
 {
@@ -259,6 +272,7 @@
 			       V3D_CLE_CT0QTS_ENABLE |
 			       job->qts);
 	}
+	v3d_mmu_flush_before_job(v3d);
 	V3D_CORE_WRITE(0, V3D_CLE_CT0QBA, job->start);
 	V3D_CORE_WRITE(0, V3D_CLE_CT0QEA, job->end);
 
@@ -310,6 +324,7 @@
 	/* Set the current and end address of the control list.
 	 * Writing the end register is what starts the job.
 	 */
+	v3d_mmu_flush_before_job(v3d);
 	V3D_CORE_WRITE(0, V3D_CLE_CT1QBA, job->start);
 	V3D_CORE_WRITE(0, V3D_CLE_CT1QEA, job->end);
 
